# Conclusions read off inferred types (`design/TYPES.md`). The parser names the type of each symbol it
# can — `Layer.mergeAll(a, b)` is `Layer<A | B, E, R>` — so what `rules/10-effect.n3` approximates
# from the constructor's first argument is exact here, and covers what that cannot see: merged,
# provided and piped layers, and layers whose type is only written in an annotation.

@prefix deus: <https://dxos.org/vocab/deus#>.
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>.

# A layer is anything typed `Layer<…>`, however it was built.
{ ?s deus:hasType ?t. ?t deus:typeHead <https://dxos.org/deus/module/effect%2FLayer#Layer> }
  => { ?s rdf:type deus:EffectLayer }.

# What it provides: `ROut`, the first type argument — one service, or each member of a union.
{ ?s deus:hasType ?t. ?t deus:typeHead <https://dxos.org/deus/module/effect%2FLayer#Layer>; deus:typeArg0 ?out.
  ?out deus:typeKind "ref"; deus:typeHead ?service }
  => { ?s deus:providesService ?service }.
{ ?s deus:hasType ?t. ?t deus:typeHead <https://dxos.org/deus/module/effect%2FLayer#Layer>; deus:typeArg0 ?out.
  ?out deus:typeKind "union"; deus:typeMember ?member. ?member deus:typeKind "ref"; deus:typeHead ?service }
  => { ?s deus:providesService ?service }.

# What it still needs: `RIn`, the third — exact, where `deus:requiresService` is a reference heuristic.
{ ?s deus:hasType ?t. ?t deus:typeHead <https://dxos.org/deus/module/effect%2FLayer#Layer>; deus:typeArg2 ?in.
  ?in deus:typeKind "ref"; deus:typeHead ?service }
  => { ?s deus:layerRequires ?service }.
{ ?s deus:hasType ?t. ?t deus:typeHead <https://dxos.org/deus/module/effect%2FLayer#Layer>; deus:typeArg2 ?in.
  ?in deus:typeKind "union"; deus:typeMember ?member. ?member deus:typeKind "ref"; deus:typeHead ?service }
  => { ?s deus:layerRequires ?service }.
